; CNAME_RECORD_MISSING, CNAME_TARGET_MISMATCH and PROVIDER_FLATTENED_CNAME.
;
; The record behind every custom subdomain: one alias at a name the customer
; owns, pointing at a host the platform issued. The reason it needs an evaluator
; rather than a lookup is `flat` below — Cloudflare and others resolve the alias
; when it is saved and serve address records in its place, so the customer did
; exactly what they were told and `dig CNAME` returns nothing at all.
;
; Telling that apart from `wrong` is only possible by resolving the target we
; issued and comparing addresses, which is why propgate-fixture.test exists.
; Keep 198.51.100.20 here in step with track.propgate-fixture.test, or `flat`
; silently stops testing flattening and starts testing a wrong address.
$ORIGIN cname.test.
$TTL 300

@       IN SOA  ns1.test. hostmaster.propgate.invalid. (
                1 7200 3600 1209600 300 )
@       IN NS   ns1.test.

; Published exactly as issued.
track.ok        IN CNAME  track.propgate-fixture.test.

; Flattened. The address is track.propgate-fixture.test's own, so this domain is
; configured correctly and must not be reported as broken — only as no longer
; following the target if the target's addresses change.
track.flat      IN A      198.51.100.20

; An address record that is not ours. Indistinguishable from `flat` without the
; target's addresses to compare against, and the opposite verdict.
track.wrong     IN A      203.0.113.5

; An alias, pointed at somebody else. Left over from the provider the customer
; migrated off, which is the usual way this happens.
track.stale     IN CNAME  track.competitor.invalid.

; The alias name does not exist. The subdomain does, so this is a live host
; missing one record.
missing         IN A      198.51.100.41
